body:
Thanks for the reproduction bars and the Profile measurements. They made this quick to pin down. What changed in PR #1429:

**Cause.** For a turning nose path, the envelope verdict's hidden-rest half measured the groove floor in world space against a path that lies in one meridian plane. It never turned the work, so the far rim across the axis read as 17 / 10 / 15.6 mm of rest, as you worked out. Once the work is turned, the near side still read about 14 mm, because it lies far below anything the Facing's nose travels.

Your Profile reading was right: the 12.49 / 12.76 figures are the same far-side reading. After turning the work, two smaller readings were left on the shaft:
- the groove floor's inner corner, which a 0.8 nose cannot reach (0.33 mm);
- the Profile Rough's own 0.25 leave on the groove floor.

**Fix.** The verdict now gets the spindle the turning kernel fitted. For turning nose ops only, a hidden top is:
- read on its orbit about the spindle;
- excused when it lies outside the axial band the nose centres travel;
- excused when the nose (grown by the op's leave) cannot touch it, using the same accessibility test the visible half already applies;
- allowed to stand by the rough's leave.

The gouge half and every mill path are unchanged.

**Results on your bars:**
- groove and groove near the top: the Facing posts;
- shaft: Facing, Profile Rough and Profile Finish all post;
- plain, stepped and wider-at-the-free-end: unchanged.

A Facing that gouges still blocks the post by name. So does a nose that rides past a groove it could have entered inside its own band.

**Not covered.** The verdict still judges reachability by the nose circle alone. A groove the nose fits into but the insert's trailing edge cannot is treated as reachable, so it errs toward refusing.
